      "findings": [
        {
          "severity": "Medium",
          "criterion": "material_exceptions",
          "location": "orientation[15]; comparison_tables[3].rows[4].cells[2]; timing_deadlines[2]; retrieval_prompts[25]; quick_review[21]; role_duties[11]",
          "problem": "Learning objective 10 promises what URAMP admission does to practice, but the section describes only the manual's 'non-disciplinary restriction' status, which means no practice, from receipt of the application and 'remains upon effective enrollment'. It then jumps to the status becoming current on successful completion. It never says that the program restores practice privileges during participation. A candidate would conclude that a participant is out of practice for the whole program, which can run up to five years.",
          "rationale": "The manual pairs its no-practice statement with a return-to-practice path during participation. 'How Does URAMP Work?' says participants entering on a substance use complaint normally require a restriction from practice for the first six months, with gradual return to work privileges. 'Return to Practice Requirements' says privileges are restored by the program before practice resumes, under a supervisor agreement. 'Application for UCAP Changes' lists return to practice after six months of compliance. Management of Disciplinary Matters, Step Four, says the board then authorizes the director to change the license status under the UCAP. The confidentiality chapter says a return to practice approved during the program goes to the licensing board for final approval. The statute likewise has the rehabilitation evaluation committee recommend whether a participant may continue or resume practice within the full scope of the license (s. 65G(d)(2)(iv)), with practice restrictions and changes subject to board approval (s. 65G(e)). The section reports one manual statement without reconciling it with the others. Fix: state that practice privileges may be restored during participation (normally after six months under a substance use agreement, individually under a mental health agreement) with board approval.",
          "authorities": [
            {
              "official_url": "https://www.mass.gov/doc/uramp-guidance-manual-pdf/download",
              "exact_section": "How Does URAMP Work?; Return to Practice Requirements; Application for UCAP Changes; Management of Disciplinary Matters, Step Four; Confidentiality Upon Successful Completion",
              "checked_date": "2026-09-16"
            },
            {
